dogscoff@eudoramail.com wrote:
> I suggested a means of creating an altar a while back. It didn't go
> down too well.
> 
> My suggested method was somewhat more difficult than the one suggested
> here:
> 
> First you had to gather/ create anything from 4 to >100 (depending in
> monster difficulty: weak monsters require more) blessed statues of your
> preferred monster type.
> 
> Then you had to arrange them in a particular pattern (which in most
> cases would require digging out most of the level) in a feature-free
> area of the dungeon.
> 
> Then you had to piss off your own god by praying and creating a temple
> (with priest) to the [monster] God. For example, if you had chosen to
> gather Orc statues, you would create a temple to the Orc God. These
> monster Gods would be a new addition to the game, but would have no
> role outside of this temple-creation procedure. Monster altars would
> behave much like any cross-aligned altar.

Why not just make the alter to the god of whatever alignment the 
creature is? If orcs are chaotic, then wouldn't the Orc God be the same 
as your god if you, too, are chaotic? (since, in any given game of 
NetHack, there are apparently only the three gods plus Moloch) Also, 
what if your actual race is orc? Then you'd DEFINATELY have the same 
god. So there's not really a need for the monster gods.

This way, you could make a temple to any god you choose, and it'd be up 
to you to choose the one you want. If it's not your own god, then yes, 
it should anger yours.

This brings up the question of how to get a priest for the temple? 
Personally, I'm in favor of taking a flesh golem and splashing some holy 
water on it. That's good enough for me. :) Or you could stone a priest 
from another temple, transport him to your new temple, and then cast 
Stone to Flesh on him. But would that convert him to the new temple's 
alignment?

